“With our head office in the UK and warehouse operations in London, Frankfurt, Miami and Singapore, we are uniquely positioned to support our customers around the world – 24 hours a day, 365 days a year,” the website read. “We keep our clients flying.” The company’s phone line is still connected but offers only endless hold music.

AOG is understood to have sold mainly smaller parts for jet engines, such as bolts, nuts, washers, dampers and seals. The parts don’t carry a serial number and are therefore harder to trace. Worryingly, some bigger parts appear to be linked to the scandal. Some turbine blades have also been identified by CFM as suspect. CFM is understood to believe that AOG had been passing off old parts for new ones. Steve Borrowdale, managing director at Multiflight, a Leeds-based engineer and parts supplier, says sneaking parts into a jet engine is difficult.","entities":["The Sydney Morning Herald","Frankfurt","London","CFM","AOG","Steve Borrowdale","Multiflight","passing off old parts for new ones","smaller parts for jet engines","turbine blades","Miami","Singapore","Leeds","The airline parts scandal sparking panic and introspection in aerospace","UK"],"facts":["ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","ASX set to open lower as Wall Street closes September with more losses -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","The best shows to stream in December -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","McRae reveals hidden message he carried under collar of his grand final shirt -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","ASX set to drop as Wall Street’s September slump deepens -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald"]}

