{"title":"ASX set to rise ahead of inflation report after big tech lifts Wall Street (The Sydney Morning Herald, 2023-10-24T20:05:06+00:00)","text":"ASX set to rise ahead of inflation report after big tech lifts Wall Street (The Sydney Morning Herald, 2023-10-24T20:05:06+00:00)\nInfratil (down 3.6 per cent), Meridian Energy (down 2.7 per cent) and Resmed (down 2 per cent) were among the biggest large-cap decliners. The lowdown The Australian sharemarket shook off a positive lead from Wall Street on Wednesday after hotter-than-expected inflation data raised the prospect of another interest rate rise at the Reserve Bank’s next meeting, which would weigh on the equity market. Interest-rate sensitive sectors, including REITS and consumer companies, were among the weakest on the local bourse as investors digested the news.\n\nGSFM investment strategist Stephen Miller said the inflation data, taken with the October Reserve Bank board meeting minutes, which revealed the board had “low tolerance for a slower return of inflation to target than currently expected”, made the possibility of an interest rate rise from the central bank in November a “near certainty”. However, commodity prices supported mining companies as iron ore prices increased 2.9 per cent overnight, bolstering Australia’s iron ore giants.","entities":["Reserve Bank","The Sydney Morning Herald","Australia","ASX","Wall Street","ASX set to rise ahead of inflation report after big tech lifts Wall Street","Infratil","Meridian Energy","Resmed","GSFM","Stephen Miller","Reserve Bank board meeting","Interest rate rise","Equity market","REITS"],"facts":["ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -MENTIONS-> Reserve Bank","Jessica Amir -SAID-> Reserve Bank","ASX set to drop as Wall Street’s September slump deepens -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","ASX set to open lower as Wall Street close