{"title":"'They seemed primed to take over': How the Great Dying doomed the 'beast tooth' and set the stage for the dawn of the dinosaurs (Live Science: The Most Interesting Articles, 2023-10-01T08:00:25+00:00)","text":"'They seemed primed to take over': How the Great Dying doomed the 'beast tooth' and set the stage for the dawn of the dinosaurs (Live Science: The Most Interesting Articles, 2023-10-01T08:00:25+00:00)
By the mid-Permian, another group of proto-mammals — the possibly warm-blooded, somewhat rodent-like therapsids — emerged and became the new dominant species. By the late Permian, they may have even developed fur. One group, known as Theriodontia (Latin for "beast tooth"), displayed a number of evolutionary innovations: A shift in the bones supporting the jaw allowed the jaw to open wider, and may have aided hearing as well. The skull and teeth became larger, the teeth more specialized, and the jaw more powerful. They seemed primed to take over. But it was not to be.

Everything changed at the Permian-Triassic boundary. Levels of CO2 spiked. That led to massive warming. Plate tectonics by now had brought all the continents together into a single giant continent — Pangea — straddled across the equator.","entities":["CO2","Live Science","Great Dying","Theriodontia","proto-mammals","therapsids","mid-Permian","late Permian","Permian-Triassic boundary","warming","plate tectonics","Pangea","equator","'They seemed primed to take over': How the Great Dying doomed the 'beast tooth' and set the stage for the dawn of the dinosaurs","dinosaurs"],"facts":["New ultrathin materials can pull climate-warming CO2 from the air -MENTIONS-> CO2","Vitalie Stavila -SAID-> CO2","We could end the AIDS epidemic in less than a decade. Here's how. -PUBLISHED_BY-> Live Science","exclusive, in-depth feature on the upcoming solar maximum -PUBLISHED_BY-> Live Science","10 scorching-hot discoveries made about the sun in 2023 -PUBLISHED_BY-> Live Science"]}

